@@s001 Minimum spanning tree: Kruskal and Prim
@@s002 ok:taken in the tree,no:discarded (cycle),dato:not yet considered
@@s003 
@@s004 I sort the edges by increasing weight: 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5. I take the first: <b>ab = 1</b>, it creates no cycles.
@@s005 <b>bc = 2</b>: connects c to the component of a–b, no cycle. Taken.
@@s006 <b>de = 2</b>: connects d–e, which is another component. Taken.
@@s007 <b>ac = 3</b>: a and c are already in the same component (a–b–c). Adding it would close a cycle: <b>discarded</b>.
@@s008 <b>cd = 4</b>: joins the two components {a,b,c} and {d,e}. Taken: now I have 4 = n − 1 edges, cost <b>9</b>. I stop.
@@s009 
@@s010 Minimum spanning tree: Kruskal and Prim
@@s011 Connecting all the nodes of a graph at the minimum total cost: the minimum spanning tree (MST) problem. Two greedy algorithms solve it: Kruskal, which adds the cheapest edges, and Prim, which grows a single tree.
@@s012 The problem
@@s013 A <b>spanning tree</b> of a connected graph is a subset of edges that connects all the nodes without cycles. It always has <code>n − 1</code> edges. The <b>minimum spanning tree</b> (MST) is the one of minimum total cost: minimum sum of the weights.
@@s014 The cost does not depend on the root: a tree has no intrinsic root. The question is only which edges to keep.
@@s015 Kruskal: take the cheapest edge
@@s016 Rule: sort the edges by increasing weight. For each edge <code>u–v</code>: if <code>u</code> and <code>v</code> are in different components, take it and merge the components; otherwise discard it, because it would close a cycle. Stop at <code>n − 1</code> edges.
@@s017 To know whether <code>u</code> and <code>v</code> are already connected one uses the <b>union-find</b> (dedicated page).
@@s018 Kruskal: order by weight and cycle test
@@s019 Why it works: the cut property
@@s020 The <b>cut property</b>: given a cut of the graph (a division of the nodes into two groups), the cheapest edge crossing the cut belongs to <i>some</i> MST. Kruskal always takes this kind of edge: the cheapest one that connects two components.
@@s021 
@@s022 Prim: grow a single tree
@@s023 Prim starts from a node and grows a single tree: at each step it adds the <b>cheapest edge leaving the tree</b> towards an external node. It is the same scheme as Dijkstra: a priority queue on the external nodes, with the minimum distance from the tree.
@@s024 
@@s025 
@@s026 Cost <code>O(m log n)</code> with a binary heap, like Kruskal. Prim is more convenient when the graph is dense; Kruskal when the edges are already sorted or when you need the global order.
@@s027 The code, side by side
@@s028 This page explains the concept. The Java code that implements it, with a line-by-line explanation and the syntax notes needed, is on the side-by-side page.
